<BLOCKQUOTE>
  <H2 class=3DHeadA>Excerpt: <BR>The SQL Optimizers</H2>
  <P class=3DBody>Whenever you execute a SQL statement, a component of =
the=20
  database known as the <EM class=3DEmphasis>optimizer</EM> must decide =
how best=20
  to access the data operated on by that statement. Oracle supports two=20
  optimizers: the rule-base optimizer (which was the original), and the=20
  cost-based optimizer. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>To figure out the optimal execution path for a =
statement, the=20
  optimizers consider the following:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The syntax you've specified for the statement =

    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>Any conditions that the data must satisfy =
(the WHERE=20
    clauses)=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The database tables your statement will need =
to access=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>All possible indexes that can be used in =
retrieving=20
    data from the table=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The Oracle RDBMS version=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The current optimizer mode=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>SQL statement hints=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>All available object statistics (generated =
via the=20
    ANALYZE command)=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The physical table location (distributed SQL) =

    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>INIT.ORA settings (parallel query, async I/O, =
etc.)=20
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <P class=3DBody>Oracle gives you a choice of two optimizing =
alternatives: the=20
  predictable rule-based optimizer and the more intelligent cost-based=20
  optimizer. </P>
  <H3 class=3DHeadB>Understanding the Rule-Based Optimizer</H3>
  <P class=3DBody>The rule-based optimizer (RBO) uses a predefined set =
of=20
  precedence rules to figure out which path it will use to access the =
database.=20
  The RDBMS kernel defaults to the rule-based optimizer under a number =
of=20
  conditions, including:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D RULE is specified in your =
INIT.ORA=20
    file=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D CHOOSE is specified in =
your INIT.ORA=20
    file, and<B class=3DEmphasisBold> </B>no statistics exist for <EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>any</EM> table involved in the statement=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>An ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D RULE =
command has=20
    been issued=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>An ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D =
CHOOSE<B=20
    class=3DEmphasisBold> </B>command has been issued, and no statistics =
exist for=20
    <EM class=3DEmphasis>any</EM> table involved in the statement=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The rule hint (e.g., SELECT /*+ RULE */. . .) =
has been=20
    used in the statement=20
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <P class=3DBody>The rule-based optimizer is driven primarily by 20 =
condition=20
  rankings, or "golden rules." These rules instruct the optimizer how to =

  determine the execution path for a statement, when to choose one index =
over=20
  another, and when to perform a full table scan. These rules, shown in =
<A=20
  class=3DXRef=20
  href=3D"http://www.oreilly.de/catalog/orsqltunpr/chapter/#73356">Table =
1</A>,=20
  are fixed, predetermined, and, in contrast with the cost-based =
optimizer, not=20
  influenced by outside sources (table volumes, index distributions, =
etc.). </P>
  <P>
  <TABLE border=3D1>
