  <P class=3DBody>While knowing the rules is helpful, they alone do not =
tell you=20
  enough about how to tune for the rule-based optimizer. To overcome =
this=20
  deficiency, the following sections provide some information that the =
rules=20
  don't tell you.</P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636806></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #1</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>Only single column indexes are ever merged. Consider =
the=20
  following SQL and indexes:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>SELECT col1, =
...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name =3D 'GURRY'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_no   =3D 127</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND dept_no  =3D 12</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (dept_no)</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_no, emp_name) </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>The SELECT statement looks at all three indexed =
columns. Many=20
  people believe that Oracle will merge the two indexes, which involve =
those=20
  three columns, to return the requested data. In fact, only the =
two-column=20
  index is used; the single-column index is not used. While Oracle <EM=20
  class=3DEmphasis>will</EM> merge two single-column indexes, it <EM=20
  class=3DEmphasis>will not</EM> merge a multi-column index with another =
index.=20
  </P>
  <P class=3DBody>There is one thing to be aware of with respect to this =
scenario.=20
  If the single-column index is a unique or primary key index, that =
would cause=20
  the single-column index to take precedence over the multi-column =
index.=20
  Compare rank 4 with rank 9 in <A class=3DXRef=20
  href=3D"http://www.oreilly.de/catalog/orsqltunpr/chapter/#73356">Table =

1</A>.</P>
  <BLOCKQUOTE>
    <P class=3DNote><B>NOTE:</B> Oracle8<EM class=3DEmphasis>i</EM> =
introduced a new=20
    hint, INDEX_JOIN, that allows you to join multi-column=20
  indexes.</P></BLOCKQUOTE>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636807></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #2</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>If all columns in an index are specified in the WHERE =
clause,=20
  that index will be used in preference to other indexes for which <EM=20
  class=3DEmphasis>some</EM> columns are referenced. For =
example:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>SELECT col1, ...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name =3D 'GURRY'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_no   =3D 127</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND dept_no  =3D 12</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (emp_name)</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_no, dept_no, cost_center) </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>In this example, only Index1 is used, because the =
WHERE clause=20
  includes all columns for that index, but does not include all columns =
for=20
  Index2. </P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636808></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #3</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>If multiple indexes can be applied to a WHERE clause, =
and they=20
  all have an equal number of columns specified, only the index created =
last=20
  will be used. For example:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>SELECT col1, =
...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name =3D 'GURRY'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_no   =3D 127</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND dept_no  =3D 12</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_category =3D 'CLERK'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (emp_name, emp_category)  Created 4pm Feb =
11<EM class=3DSuperscript>th</EM> 2002</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_no, dept_no) Created 5pm Feb 11<EM =
class=3DSuperscript>th</EM> 2002</CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>In this example, only Index2 is used, because it was =
created at=20
  5 p.m. and the other index was created at 4 p.m. This behavior can =
pose a=20
  problem, because if you rebuild indexes in a different order than they =
were=20
  first created, a different index may suddenly be used for your =
queries. To=20
  deal with this problem, many sites have a naming standard requiring =
that=20
  indexes are named in alphabetical order as they are created. Then, if =
a table=20
  is rebuilt, the indexes can be rebuilt in alphabetical order, =
preserving the=20
  correct creation order. You could, for example, number your indexes. =
Each new=20
  index added to a table would then be given the next number.</P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636809></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #4</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>If multiple columns of an index are being accessed =
with an =3D=20
  operator, that will override other operators such as LIKE or BETWEEN. =
Two =3D's=20
  will override two =3D's and a LIKE. For example:</P><PRE><CODE =
class=3DCode>SELECT col1, ...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name  LIKE 'GUR%'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_no       =3D 127</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND dept_no      =3D 12</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_category =3D 'CLERK'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_class    =3D 'C1'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (emp_category, emp_class, emp_name)  </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_no, dept_no) </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>In this example, only Index2 is utilized despite =
Index1 having=20
  three columns accessed and Index2 having only two column accessed. =
</P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636810></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #5</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>A higher percentage of columns accessed will override =
a lower=20
  percentage of columns accessed. So generally, the optimizer will =
choose to use=20
  the index from which you specify the highest percentage of columns. =
However,=20
  as stated previously, all columns specified in a unique or primary key =
index=20
  will override the use of all other indexes. For example:</P><PRE><CODE =
class=3DCode>SELECT col1, ...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name     =3D 'GURRY'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_no       =3D 127</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>   AND emp_class    =3D 'C1'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (emp_name, emp_class, emp_category)  </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_no, dept_no) </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>In this example, only Index1 is utilized, because 66% =
of the=20
  columns are accessed. Index2 is not used because a lesser 50% of the =
indexed=20
  columns are used.</P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636811></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #6</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>If you join two tables, the rule-based optimizer needs =
to select=20
  a driving table. The table selected can have a significant impact on=20
  performance, particularly when the optimizer decides to use nested =
loops. A=20
  row will be returned from the driving table, and then the matching =
rows=20
  selected from the other table. It is important that as few rows as =
possible=20
  are selected from the driving table. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>The rule-based optimizer uses the following rules to =
select the=20
  driving table:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>A unique or primary key index will always =
cause the=20
    associated table to be selected as the driving table in front of a=20
    non-unique or non-primary key index.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>An index for which you apply the equality =
operator (=3D)=20
    to all columns will take precedence over indexes from which you use =
only=20
    some columns, and will result in the underlying table being chosen =
as the=20
    driving table for the query.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The table that has a higher percentage of =
columns in an=20
    index will override the table that has a lesser percentage of =
columns=20
    indexed.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>A table that satisfies one two-column index =
in the=20
    WHERE clause of a query will be chosen as the driving table in front =
of a=20
    table that satisfies two single-column indexes.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>If two tables have the same number of index =
columns=20
    satisfied, the table that is listed last in the FROM clause will be =
the=20
    driving table. In the SQL below, the EMP table will be the driving =
table=20
    because it is listed last in the FROM clause.=20
    <P><PRE><CLASS=3D"FM-CODE">SELECT ....
<CLASS=3D"FM-CODE">  FROM DEPT d, EMP e
<CLASS=3D"FM-CODE"> WHERE e.emp_name    =3D 'GURRY'
<CLASS=3D"FM-CODE">   AND d.dept_name   =3D 'FINANCE'
<CLASS=3D"FM-CODE">   AND d.dept_no     =3D e.dept_no
</PRE></LI></UL>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636812></A>What the RBO rules don't =
tell you=20
  #7</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>If a WHERE clause has a column that is the leading =
column on any=20
  index, the rule-based optimizer will use that index. The exception is =
if a=20
  function is placed on the leading index column in the WHERE clause. =
For=20
  example:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>SELECT col1, ...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE emp_name     =3D 'GURRY'</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index1 (emp_name, emp_class, emp_category) </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>Index2 (emp_class, emp_name, emp_category) </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>Index1 will be used, because emp_name (used in the =
WHERE clause)=20
  is the leading column. Index2 will not be used, because emp_name is =
not the=20
  leading column. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>The following example illustrates what happens when a =
function=20
  is applied to an indexed column:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>SELECT =
col1, ...</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>  FROM emp</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode> WHERE LTRIM(emp_name) =3D 'GURRY' </CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>In this case, because the LTRIM function has been =
applied to the=20
  column, <EM class=3DEmphasis>no</EM> index will be used.</P>
  <H3 class=3DHeadB>Understanding the Cost-Based Optimizer</H3>
  <P class=3DBody>The<B class=3DEmphasisBold> </B>cost-based optimizer =
is a more=20
  sophisticated facility than the rule-based optimizer. To determine the =
best=20
  execution path for a statement, it uses database information such as =
table=20
  size, number of rows, key spread, and so forth, rather than rigid =
rules. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>The information required by the cost-based optimizer =
is=20
  available once a table has been analyzed via the ANALYZE command, or =
via the=20
  DBMS_STATS facility. If a table has not been analyzed, the cost-based=20
  optimizer can use only rule-based logic to select the best access =
path. It is=20
  possible to run a schema with a combination of cost-based and =
rule-based=20
  behavior by having some tables analyzed and others not analyzed.</P>
  <BLOCKQUOTE>
    <P class=3DNote><B>NOTE:</B> The ANALYZE command and the DBMS_STATS =
functions=20
    collect statistics about tables, clusters, and indexes, and store =
those=20
    statistics in the data dictionary. </P></BLOCKQUOTE>
  <P class=3DBody>A SQL statement will default to the cost-based =
optimizer if any=20
  one of the tables involved in the statement has been analyzed. The =
cost-based=20
  optimizer then makes an educated guess as to the best access path for =
the=20
  other tables based on information in the data dictionary. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>The RDBMS kernel defaults to using the cost-based =
optimizer=20
  under a number of situations, including the following:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D CHOOSE has been specified =
in the=20
    INIT.ORA file, and statistics exist for at least one table involved =
in the=20
    statement=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>An ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D =
CHOOSE command=20
    has been executed, and statistics exist for at least one table =
involved in=20
    the statement=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>An ALTER SESSION SET OPTIMIZER_MODE =3D =
FIRST_ROWS (or=20
    ALL_ROWS) command has been executed, and statistics exist for at =
least one=20
    table involved in the statement=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>A statement uses the FIRST_ROWS or ALL_ROWS =
hint (e.g.,=20
    SELECT /*+ FIRST_ROWS */. . .)=20
    <P></P></LI></UL>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636814></A>ANALYZE command</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>The way that you analyze your tables can have a =
dramatic effect=20
  on your SQL performance. If your DBA forgets to analyze tables or =
indexes=20
  after a table re-build, the impact on performance can be devastating. =
If your=20
  DBA analyzes each weekend, a new threshold may be reached and Oracle =
may=20
  change its execution plan. The new plan will more often than not be an =

  improvement, but will occasionally be worse.</P>
  <P class=3DBody>I cannot stress enough that if every SQL statement has =
been=20
  tuned, do <EM class=3DEmphasis>not</EM> analyze just for the sake of =
it. One=20
  site that I tuned had a critical SQL statement that returned data in =
less than=20
  a second. The DBA analyzed each weekend believing that the execution =
path=20
  would continue to improve. One Monday, morning I got a phone call =
telling me=20
  that the response time had risen to 310 seconds. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>If you do want to analyze frequently, use=20
  DBMS_STATS.EXPORT_SCHEMA_STATS to back up the existing statistics =
prior to=20
  re-analyzing. This gives you the ability to revert back to the =
previous=20
  statistics if things screw up. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>When you analyze, you can have Oracle look at all rows =
in a=20
  table (ANALYZE COMPUTE) or at a sampling of rows (ANALYZE ESTIMATE).=20
  Typically, I use ANALYZE ESTIMATE for very large tables (1,000,000 =
rows or=20
  more), and ANALYZE COMPUTE for small to medium tables. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>I strongly recommend that you analyze FOR ALL INDEXED =
COLUMNS=20
  for any table that can have severe data skewness. For example, if a =
large=20
  percentage of rows in a table has the same value in a given column, =
that=20
  represents skewness. The FOR ALL INDEXED COLUMNS option makes the =
cost-based=20
  optimizer aware of the skewness of a column's data in addition to the=20
  cardinality (number-distinct values) of that data. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>When a table is analyzed using ANALYZE, all associated =
indexes=20
  are analyzed as well. If an index is subsequently dropped and =
recreated, it=20
  must be re-analyzed. Be aware that the procedures=20
  DBMS_STATS.GATHER_SCHEMA_STATS and GATHER_TABLE_STATS analyze only =
tables by=20
  default, not their indexes. When using those procedures, you must =
specify the=20
  CASCADE=3D&gt;TRUE option for indexes to be analyzed as well.</P>
  <P class=3DBody>Following are some sample ANALYZE =
statements:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>ANALYZE TABLE EMP ESTIMATE =
STATISTICS SAMPLE 5 PERCENT FOR ALL INDEXED COLUMNS;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>ANALYZE INDEX EMP_NDX1 ESTIMATE STATISTICS SAMPLE 5 =
PERCENT FOR ALL INDEXED COLUMNS;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>ANALYZE TABLE EMP COMPUTE STATISTICS FOR ALL INDEXED =
COLUMNS;</CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>If you analyze a table by mistake, you can delete the=20
  statistics. For example:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode>ANALYZE TABLE EMP =
DELETE STATISTICS;</CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>Analyzing can take an excessive amount of time if you =
use the=20
  COMPUTE option on large objects. We find that on almost every =
occasion,=20
  ANALYZE ESTIMATE 5 PERCENT on a large table forces the optimizer make =
the same=20
  decision as ANALYZE COMPUTE.</P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636815></A>Tuning prior to =
releasing to=20
  production</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>A major dilemma that exists with respect to the =
cost-based=20
  optimizer (CBO) is how to tune the SQL for production prior to it =
being=20
  released. Most development and test databases will contain =
substantially fewer=20
  rows than a production database. It is therefore highly likely that =
the CBO=20
  will make different decisions on execution plans. Many sites can't =
afford the=20
  cost and inconvenience of copying the production database into a=20
  pre-production database. </P>
  <P class=3DBody>Oracle8<EM class=3DEmphasis>i</EM> and later provides =
various=20
  features to overcome this problem, including DBMS_STATS and the =
outline=20
  facility. Each is explained in more detail later in this book. </P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636817></A>Inner workings of the =
cost-based=20
  optimizer</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>Unlike the rule-based optimizer, the cost-based =
optimizer does=20
  not have hard and fast path evaluation rules. The cost-based optimizer =
is=20
  flexible and can adapt to its environment. This adaptation is possible =
only=20
  once the necessary underlying object statistics have been refreshed=20
  (re-analyzed). What is constant is the method by which the cost-based=20
  optimizer calculates each possible execution plan and evaluates its =
cost=20
  (efficiency).</P>
  <P class=3DBody>The cost-based optimizer's functionality can be =
(loosely) broken=20
  into the following steps:</P>
  <OL>
    <LI class=3DListNumber>Parse the SQL (check syntax, object =
privileges, etc.).=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListNumber>Generate a list of <EM =
class=3DEmphasis>all</EM>=20
    potential execution plans.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListNumber>Calculate (estimate) the cost of each =
execution plan=20
    using all available object statistics.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListNumber>Select the execution plan with the<EM =
class=3DEmphasis>=20
    </EM>lowest cost<EM class=3DEmphasis>.</EM>=20
    <P></P></LI></OL>
  <P class=3DBody>The cost-based optimizer will be used only if at least =
one table=20
  within a SQL statement has statistics (table statistics for unanalyzed =
tables=20
  are estimated). If no statistics are available for any table involved =
in the=20
  SQL, the RDBMS will resort to the rule-based optimizer, unless the =
cost-based=20
  optimizer is forced via statement-level HINTS or by an optimizer goal =
of=20
  ALL_ROWS or FIRST_ROWS.</P>
  <P class=3DBody>To understand how the cost-based optimizer works and,=20
  ultimately, how to exploit it, we need to understand how it thinks. =
</P>
  <DL>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Primary key and/or UNIQUE index =
equality=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>A UNIQUE index's selectivity is recognized =
as 100%.=20
    No other indexed access method is more precise. For this reason, a =
unique=20
    index is always used when available.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Non-UNIQUE index equality=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>For non-UNIQUE indexes, index selectivity =
is=20
    calculated. The cost-based optimizer makes the assumption that the =
table=20
    (and subsequent indexes) have uniform data spread unless you use the =
FOR ALL=20
    INDEXED COLUMNS option of the ANALYZE. That option will make the =
cost-based=20
    optimizer aware of how the data in the indexed columns is skewed.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Range evaluation=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>For index range execution plans, =
selectivity is=20
    evaluated. This evaluation is based on a column's most recent =
high-value and=20
    low-value statistics. Again, the cost-based optimizer makes the =
assumption=20
    that the table (and subsequent indexes) have uniform data spread =
unless you=20
    use the FOR ALL INDEXED COLUMNS option when analyzing the table.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Range evaluation over bind variables=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>For index range execution plans, =
selectivity is=20
    guessed. Prior to Oracle9<EM class=3DEmphasis>i</EM>, because bind =
variable=20
    values are not available at parse time (values are passed to the =
cursor=20
    after the execution plan has been decided), the optimizer cannot =
make=20
    decisions based on bind variable values. The optimizer assumes a =
rule of=20
    thumb of 25% selectivity for unbounded bind variable ranges (e.g., =
WHERE=20
    dept_no =3D :b1) and 50% selectivity for bounded ranges (WHERE =
dept_no &gt;=20
    :b1 AND dept_no &lt; :b2). Beginning with Oracle9<EM =
class=3DEmphasis>i</EM>,=20
    the cost-based optimizer obtains bind variable values prior to =
determining=20
    an execution plan.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Histograms=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>Prior to the introduction of histograms in =
Oracle=20
    7.3, The cost-based optimizer could not distinguish grossly uneven =
key data=20
    spreads<EM class=3DEmphasis>.</EM>=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>System resource usage=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>By default, the cost-based optimizer =
assumes that you=20
    are the only person accessing the database. Oracle9<EM =
class=3DEmphasis>i</EM>=20
    gives you the ability to store information about system resource =
usage, and=20
    can make much better informed decisions based on workload (read up =
on the=20
    DBMS_STATS.GATHER_SYSTEM_STATS package).=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Current statistics are important=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>The cost-based optimizer can make poor =
execution plan=20
    choices when a table has been analyzed but its indexes have not =
been, or=20
    when indexes have been analyzed but not the tables.=20
    <P></P>
    <DD class=3DListVariable->You should not force the database to use =
the=20
    cost-based optimizer via inline hints when no statistics are =
available for=20
    any table involved in the SQL.=20
    <P></P>
    <DD class=3DListVariable->Using old <EM =
class=3DEmphasis>(</EM>obsolete<EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>)</EM> statistics can be more dangerous than =
estimating the=20
    statistics at runtime, but keep in mind that changing statistics =
frequently=20
    can also blow up in your face, particularly on a mission-critical =
system=20
    with lots of online users. Always back up your statistics before you =

    re-analyze by using DBMS_STATS.EXPORT_SCHEMA_STATS.=20
    <P></P>
    <DD class=3DListVariable->Analyzing large tables and their =
associated indexes=20
    with the COMPUTE option will take a long, long time, requiring lots =
of CPU,=20
    I/O, and temporary tablespace resources. It is often overkill. =
Analyzing=20
    with a consistent value, for example, estimate 3%, will usually =
allow the=20
    cost-based optimizer to make optimal decisions=20
    <P></P></DD></DL>
  <P class=3DBody>Combining the information provided by the selectivity =
rules with=20
  other database I/O information allows the cost-based optimizer to =
calculate=20
  the cost of an execution plan. </P>
  <H4 class=3DHeadC><A name=3D_Toc523636818></A>EXPLAIN PLAN for the =
cost-based=20
  optimizer</H4>
  <P class=3DBody>Oracle provides information on the cost of query =
execution via=20
  the EXPLAIN PLAN facility. EXPLAIN PLAN can be used to display the =
calculated=20
  execution cost(s) via some extensions to the utility. In particular, =
the plan=20
  table's COST column returns a value that increases or decreases to =
show the=20
  relative cost of a query. For example:</P><PRE><CODE class=3DCode><EM =
class=3DCodeUserInput>EXPLAIN PLAN FOR </EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>  SELECT count(*) =
</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>    FROM winners, =
horses</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>   WHERE =
winners.owner=3Dhorses.owner</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>     AND winners.horse_name =
LIKE 'Mr %'</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>    </EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>COLUMN "SQL" FORMAT =
a56</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>        </EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>SELECT lpad(' =
',2*level)||operation||''  </EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>        ||options ||' =
'||object_name||</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>       decode(OBJECT_TYPE, =
'', '', </EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>           =
'('||object_type||')') "SQL",</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>       cost "Cost", =
cardinality "Num Rows"</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>  FROM   =
plan_table</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>CONNECT BY prior id =3D =
parent_id</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode><EM class=3DCodeUserInput>  START WITH id =3D =
0;</EM></CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>&nbsp;</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>SQL                            Cost   Num Rows</CODE>
<CODE =
class=3DCode>-----------------------------------------------</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>SELECT STATEMENT                 44          1</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>    SORT AGGREGATE                                    =
                   </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>      HASH JOIN                  44     100469</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>        INDEX RANGE SCAN MG1(NON-UNIQUE)              =
        </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>                                  2       1471</CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>        INDEX FAST FULL SCAN OWNER_PK(UNIQUE)         =
           </CODE>
<CODE class=3DCode>                                  4       6830</CODE>
</PRE>
  <P class=3DBody>By manipulating the cost-based optimizer (i.e., via =
inline=20
  hints, by creating/removing indexes, or by adjusting the way that =
indexes or=20
  tables are analyzed), we can see the differences in the execution cost =
as=20
  calculated by the optimizer. Use EXPLAIN PLAN to look at different =
variations=20
  on a query, and choose the variation with the lowest relative =
cost.</P>
  <P class=3DBody>For absolute optimal performance, many sites have the =
majority=20
  of the tables and indexes analyzed but a small number of tables that =
are used=20
  in isolation are not analyzed. This is usually to force rule-based =
behavior on=20
  the tables that are not analyzed. However, it is important that tables =
that=20
  have not been analyzed are not joined with tables that have been =
analyzed.=20
</P>
  <H3 class=3DHeadB>Some Common Optimizer Misconceptions</H3>
  <P class=3DBody>Let's clear up some common misconceptions regarding =
the=20
  optimizers:</P>
  <DL>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Oracle8i and Oracle9i don't support the =

    rule-based optimizer=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>This is totally false. Certain publications =
mentioned=20
    this some time ago, but Oracle now assures us that this is =
definitely <EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>not</EM> true.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>Hints can't be used with the rule-based =
optimizer=20

    <DD class=3DListVariable>The large majority of hints can indeed be =
applied to=20
    SQL statements using the rule-based optimizer.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>SQL tuned for rule will run well in =
cost=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>If you are very lucky it may, but when you =
transfer=20
    to cost, expect a handful of SQL statements that require tuning. =
However,=20
    there is not a single site that I have transferred and been unable =
to tune.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>SQL tuned for cost will run well in =
rule=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>This is highly unlikely, unless the code =
was written=20
    with knowledge of the rule-based optimizer.=20
    <P></P>
    <DT class=3DListVariableTerm>You can't run rule and cost together=20
    <DD class=3DListVariable>You can run both together by setting the =
INIT.ORA=20
    parameter OPTIMIZER_MODE to CHOOSE, and having some tables analyzed =
and=20
    others not. Be careful that you don't join tables that <EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>are</EM> analyzed with tables that are <EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>not</EM> analyzed.=20
    <P></P></DD></DL>
  <H3 class=3DHeadB>Which Optimizer to Use?</H3>
  <P class=3DBody>If you are currently using the rule-based optimizer, I =
strongly=20
  recommend that you transfer to the cost-based optimizer. Here is a =
list of the=20
  reasons why:</P>
  <UL>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The time spent coding is reduced.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>Coders do not need to be aware of the rules.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>There are more features, and far more tuning =
tools,=20
    available for the cost-based optimizer.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The chances of third-party packages =
performing well has=20
    been improved considerably. Many third-party packages are written to =
run on=20
    DB2, Informix, and SQL*Server, as well as on Oracle. The code has =
<EM=20
    class=3DEmphasis>not</EM> been written to suit the rule-based =
optimizer; it=20
    has been written in a generic fashion.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>End users can develop tuned code without =
having to=20
    learn a large set of optimizer rules.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>The cost-based optimizer has improved =
dramatically from=20
    one version of Oracle to the next. Development of the rule-based =
optimizer=20
    is stalled.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>There is less risk from adding new indexes.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>There are many features that are available =
only with=20
    the cost-based optimizer. These features include recognition of =
materialized=20
    views, star transformation, the use of function indexes, and so on. =
The=20
    number of such features is huge, and as time goes on, the gap =
between cost=20
    and rule will widen.=20
    <P></P>
    <LI class=3DListBullet>Oracle has introduced features such as the =
DBMS_STATS=20
    package and outlines to get around known problems with the =
inconsistency of=20
    the cost-based optimizer across environments.=20
    <P></P></LI></UL></BLOCKQUOTE><!-- End of sample chapter -->
